WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.640
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:08.960
If you have been watching this series for&nbsp;
a while, you know very well that I love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:15.040
learning algorithms and fluid simulations.&nbsp;
But do you know what I like even better?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:22.480
Learning algorithms applied to fluid simulations,&nbsp;
so I couldn’t be happier with today’s paper.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.920
We can create wondrous fluid simulations like&nbsp;
the ones you see here by studying the laws of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.760
fluid motion from physics, and writing a&nbsp;
computer program that contains these laws.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:38.160
However, I just mentioned learning algorithms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:44.320
How do these even come to the picture? If we&nbsp;
can write a program that simulates the laws,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.360
why would we need learning-based algorithms?&nbsp;
This doesn’t seem to make any sense.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.800
You see, in this task, the neural networks&nbsp;
can be also applied to solve something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:01.440
we already know how to solve. However, if we&nbsp;
use a neural network to perform this task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.280
we have to train it, which is a long and&nbsp;
arduous process. I hope to have convinced you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:14.320
that this is a bad, bad idea. Why would anyone&nbsp;
bother to do that? Does this make any sense?

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:20.560
Well, it does make a lot of sense! And the&nbsp;
reason for that is that this training step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:26.880
only has to be done once, and afterwards,&nbsp;
querying the neural network, that is, predicting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.680
what happens next in the simulation runs almost&nbsp;
immediately. This takes way less time than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:40.320
calculating all the forces and pressures in the&nbsp;
simulation while retaining high quality results.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:45.600
This earlier work from last year&nbsp;
absolutely nailed this problem. Look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.400
this is a scene with the&nbsp;
boxes it has been trained on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:55.200
And now, let’s ask it to try to simulate the&nbsp;
evolution of significantly different shapes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.320
Wow. It not only does well with&nbsp;
these previously unseen shapes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.360
but it also handles their&nbsp;
interactions really well.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.480
But there was more! We could also train it&nbsp;
on a tiny domain with only a few particles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.400
and then, it was able to learn general&nbsp;
concepts that we can reuse to simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.000
a much bigger domain, and also,&nbsp;
with more particles. Fantastic!

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.680
This was a simple, general model that&nbsp;
truly is a force to be reckoned with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.320
Now, this is a great leap in neural network-based&nbsp;
physics simulations, but of course, not everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:42.560
was perfect there. For instance, over longer&nbsp;
timeframes, solids became incorrectly deformed.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.760
And now, a newer iteration of a similar system&nbsp;
just came out from DeepMind’s research lab that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.720
promises to extend these neural networks&nbsp;
for an incredible set of use cases:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.880
aerodynamics, structural mechanics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:06.160
cloth simulations and more. I am very&nbsp;
excited to see how far they have come since!

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:12.640
So let’s see how well it does, first, with&nbsp;
rollouts, then, with generalization experiments.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:19.840
Here is the first rollout experiment, so what&nbsp;
does that mean, and what are we seeing here?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:26.160
On the left, you see a verified handcrafted&nbsp;
algorithm performing the simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.240
we will accept this as the true data, and&nbsp;
on the right, the AI is trying to continue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:39.920
the initial simulation. But there is one problem.&nbsp;
And that problem is that the AI was only trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:47.360
on short simulations with 400 time steps,&nbsp;
that’s only a few seconds! And unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:54.800
this test will be a hundred times longer. So, it&nbsp;
only learned on short simulations, can it manage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:04.800
to run a longer one and remain stable? Well, that&nbsp;
will be tough…but, so far so good…still running.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:11.920
My goodness, this is really something. Still&nbsp;
running and it’s very close to the ground truth!

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:20.560
Okay, that is fantastic, but that was just a&nbsp;
piece of cloth. What about interaction with other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:27.840
objects? Well, let’s see. I’ll stop the process&nbsp;
here and there so we can inspect the differences.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:31.680
Again, flying colors. Loving it.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.080
And, apparently, the same can be said for&nbsp;
simulations in structural mechanics, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:44.800
incompressible fluid dynamics. Now, that is one&nbsp;
more important lesson here - to be able to solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:51.040
such a wide variety of simulation problems, we&nbsp;
need a bunch of different hancrafted algorithms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:58.800
that took many-many years to develop. But this&nbsp;
one neural network can learn and perform them all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.000
and it can do it 10 to a 100 times quicker.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:09.120
And now comes the second half, generalization&nbsp;
experiments. This means a simulation scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:15.280
with shapes that the algorithm has never seen&nbsp;
before. And let’s see if it obtained general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:22.800
knowledge of the underlying laws of physics&nbsp;
to be able to pull this off. Oh my! Look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:29.360
that! Even the tiny piece that is hanging off&nbsp;
of the flag is simulated nearly perfectly.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:35.920
In this one, they gave it different wind speeds&nbsp;
and directions that it hadn’t seen before, and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:41.360
only that, but we are varying these parameters&nbsp;
in time, and it doesn’t even break a sweat.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:47.120
And hold on to your papers, because&nbsp;
here comes my favorite - it can even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.760
learn on a small-scale simulation with&nbsp;
a simple rectangular flag, and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:01.520
we throw at it a much more detailed, cylindrical&nbsp;
flag with tassels. Surely this will be way beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:10.960
what any learning algorithm can do today.&nbsp;
And…okay, come on…I am truly out of words. Look.

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:18.800
So now, this is official. We can ask an AI to&nbsp;
perform something that we already know how to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:25.440
and it will not only be able to reproduce&nbsp;
similar simulations, but we can even ask things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:31.600
that were previously quite unreasonably outside&nbsp;
of what it had seen, and it handles all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:38.800
with flying colors. And it does this much better&nbsp;
than previous techniques were able to. And it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:45.600
learn from multiple different algorithms at&nbsp;
the same time. Wow. What a time to be alive!

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.720
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

